# Kill Line Theme Builder – Full Manual  
### Explain Like I’m Five, Facility Edition

This manual is for the moment when you open the app, look at all the panels, and your brain goes:

> “I have no idea what any of this is for.”

You have:

- a builder  
- a theme system  
- motion  
- depth  
- sound  
- outside machines  

You might not know what a “theme” is, what you’re trying to make, or what these knobs do.

This file is here to:

- explain everything in **plain language**  
- show you **how to play** with the toys  
- keep you in the **industrial world** you described (Stock Yards, rail, factory, unsentimental)  

You can read it straight through, or jump around by headings.


---

## 1. What This Thing Actually Is

### 1.1 Two big pieces: stuff vs. how stuff looks

Every page or screen has two big parts:

1. **The stuff**  
   - the words  
   - the boxes  
   - the buttons  
   - any icons or diagrams  

2. **The way the stuff looks and behaves**  
   - colors  
   - fonts  
   - how big things are  
   - how far apart things are  
   - what the background looks like  
   - whether anything moves, or stays still  
   - how it reacts to sound, depth, or outside apps  

The **theme** is part 2.

The **builder** is a machine that lets you control part 2 without writing code every time.

You bring:

- ideas  
- language  
- the world (Stock Yards, factory, rails)  

The builder:

- gives you knobs and blocks so that world can show up on the screen consistently.


### 1.2 What is a “theme” in this context?

Put simply:

> A theme is a set of rules about how things look and move.

The rules answer questions like:

- What kind of colors do we use here?  
- How do headers look?  
- How do warning areas look?  
- How much dirt and damage is there?  
- Do things wiggle or stay rigid?  
- What feels close, what feels far?  
- How does sound show up visually, if it does at all?

The theme builder lets you **set those rules visually** instead of hard-coding them in CSS or JS.

Once you have those rules, you can:

- build a lot of pages  
- reuse the same rules  
- keep everything in the same world


### 1.3 Your specific world: not candy, not clean, not cute

You already set the tone:

- Chicago Union Stock Yards  
- Back of the Yards  
- rails, freight, switching  
- factories, processing, slaughter, distribution  
- unsentimental, industrial, workmanlike  
- sound as cargo, not magic  

So the theme here is not:

- bright pastel buttons  
- soft rounded everything  
- cheerful copy  

It is more like:

- concrete floors  
- bolted steel  
- rust stains  
- faded yellow signage  
- blueprint maps and yard diagrams  
- hazard icons  
- motion like machines cycling, not toys bouncing  

This manual assumes that world.

Whenever you make a choice, you can ask:

> “Would this show up in a yard, a plant, or a control room?”


