## 4. The Fancy Panels, Explained Simple

You have extra panels:

- **Live Background** (WebGL)  
- **Front And Back** (Depth / LiDAR)  
- **Sound Pushes Things** (Pure Data / audio)  
- **Other Machines Steer This** (TouchDesigner)  
- **Play With Code** (creative coding)  

This section explains each one in basic terms and how they connect to what you already did.

---

### 4.1 Live Background – moving wallpaper

Think of this as **moving wallpaper** behind everything.

You can switch from:

- a still background  
to  
- a background that slowly moves.

In the **Live Background** panel, you see:

- **Background Style**  
  Options like:
  - Still Picture  
  - Soft Moving Fog  
  - Moving Lines  
  - Sparks In The Dark  

- **How Much Movement**  
  How strong the movement is.

- **How Sharp Or Soft**  
  Whether the movement is crisp or blurred.

- **Use Depth If There Is Any**  
  If you have a depth picture, this lets the background feel deeper.

You do not have to know WebGL to use it.

You just pick:

- type of motion  
- strength  
- softness  

You use it when:

- you want the screen to feel alive  
- but you do not want to add a lot of foreground motion  

Most of the time, subtle, slow movement is enough:

- small flow of fog  
- thin lines sliding slowly  
- faint flickers far in the back  


### 4.2 Front And Back – close vs far

Depth is about **distance**.

If you have a special kind of image (from LiDAR or a depth camera), the app can:

- tell which parts are near  
- which parts are middle  
- which are far  

From the **Front And Back** panel:

- **Pick A Depth Picture**  
  Choose a photo that carries depth information.

- **Make Layers**  
  Split it into:
  - front stuff  
  - middle stuff  
  - far stuff  

- Toggle:
  - Show Only Front Stuff  
  - Show Only Middle Stuff  
  - Show Only Far Stuff  

- Adjust:
  - **Make Front Pop** – sharpen/brighten near stuff.  
  - **Blur The Back** – blur far stuff.  
  - **How Much Fake 3D** – slight tilt or parallax when you move/scroll.

Why this helps:

- The important things can be **sharp and close**.  
- The less important things can be **soft and far**.  

This matches your world:

- the work zone in front  
- the rest of the yard fading into the distance  


### 4.3 Sound Pushes Things – sound into motion

Sound can be measured:

- how loud in low pitch  
- how loud in middle  
- how loud in high  

Those numbers can drive movement and color.

The **Sound Pushes Things** panel gives you:

- **Use Sound To Move Things** – master on/off.  
- **Pick Sound** – what input to use (file, mic, Pd output).  
- Live bars:
  - Low Booms  
  - Middle Stuff  
  - High Hiss  

- Mappings:
  - Make Things Bounce – vertical movement.  
  - Make Colors Pulse – saturation/brightness changes.  
  - Make Lights Flicker – brightness flicker.

- **How Strong** – overall strength.

Use cases:

- Show beats as pulses in the Kill Line.  
- Make hazard indicators flicker with high-frequency noise.  
- Let low booms gently push main boxes up/down.

You decide what moves.  
You decide how dramatic it is.


### 4.4 Other Machines Steer This – TouchDesigner

TouchDesigner can:

- look at cameras  
- look at sound  
- create visuals  
- output numbers and signals  

Your builder can:

- subscribe to those numbers  
- use them to move things and change colors  

In the **Other Machines Steer This** panel:

- **Connect To Big Visual Machine**  
  Tells the app to link to TouchDesigner via the bridge.

- A list of **channels** from TouchDesigner:
  - `BeatLight`  
  - `CameraMove`  
  - `NoiseLevel`  
  - etc.

- A list of **targets** in the builder:
  - Move Background  
  - Move Boxes  
  - Move Colors  

You can:

- drag a channel onto a target.  

Example:

- `BeatLight` → `Move Boxes`  
  Boxes wiggle or pop when the beat hits.

- `CameraMove` → `Move Background`  
  Live background shifts when the camera sees motion.

This lets your theme become:

- not just a static factory diagram  
- but a live board responding to external systems  


### 4.5 Play With Code – your own generators

The **Play With Code** panel is a tiny coding workshop.

You get:

- a text area where you type small JS snippets  
- a preview canvas that shows what the code draws  
- a switch for plain vs “3D” drawing modes  
- a Run button  

You can write tiny pieces like:

- draw lines that look like rail diagrams  
- draw moving stripes like scanning beams  
- draw small grids or dots like distant factory lights  

Even without heavy programming, you can:

- copy examples  
- tweak numbers  
- change colors  
- see what happens  

These code outputs can then be used as:

- backgrounds  
- overlays  
- stickers  

So your theme is not just made from downloaded assets, but also from patterns that only you generate.


